---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { DocCards, Callout, StepHeading, PrereqCard, FeedbackFooter } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const breadcrumbs = [{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Getting Started' }];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <header class="docHero">
    <p class="docEyebrow">Quick Start Guide</p>
    <h1>Getting Started</h1>
    <p class="docLead">
      OpenFlows is an autonomous AI software team that turns GitHub issues into reviewed,
      production-ready pull requests, running entirely inside your own self-hosted Coder environment.
      It stands up a small team of specialized agents that plan, write, test, and adversarially review
      code on your behalf. You stay in control of the important decisions; the team handles the
      execution.
    </p>
    <div class="docMeta">
      <span class="docMetaItem">
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>
        ~10 min read
      </span>
      <span class="docMetaSep"></span>
      <span class="docMetaItem">Beginner</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <Callout type="tip" title="The one-line idea">
    You open a GitHub issue. OpenFlows plans it, writes it, stresses it, and merges it - then tells
    you what happened. <strong>Coder governs <em>where</em> agents run; OpenFlows governs
    <em>how</em> they coordinate.</strong>
  </Callout>

  <StepHeading step="1" id="who-it-is-for">Who it is for</StepHeading>
  <p>
    OpenFlows is for developers, companies, and stakeholders who want AI to execute end-to-end with
    guardrails inside their existing engineering workflow:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Developers</strong> who want the mechanics of planning, coding, testing, and review handled while they stay in charge of the important decisions.</li>
    <li><strong>Companies</strong> that want governed, auditable AI delivery without exposing model keys or weakening security.</li>
    <li><strong>Stakeholders</strong> who want a faster, more transparent path from "we want this feature" to "it shipped."</li>
  </ul>

  <StepHeading step="2" id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</StepHeading>
  <div class="prereqGrid">
    <PrereqCard
      name="Self-hosted Coder"
      detail="OpenFlows rides on your Coder server, which provides identity, model access, and the disposable workspaces where agents run."
    />
    <PrereqCard
      name="GitHub repository"
      detail="OpenFlows turns GitHub issues into pull requests, so it needs a repository and GitHub authentication for it."
    />
    <PrereqCard
      name="A coordination store"
      detail="A shared record of truth (Redis) that coordinates the team, plus a database for Coder itself."
    />
    <PrereqCard
      name="GitHub OAuth"
      detail="OpenFlows acts on GitHub through a real identity linked via GitHub OAuth, not a shared token."
    />
  </div>

  <Callout type="info" title="Coder governs where agents run; OpenFlows governs how they coordinate">
    The two systems divide the work deliberately:
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Coder</strong> decides where work runs. It provides governed, isolated, disposable
      workspaces and a central model gateway where every AI call happens. Worker workspaces hold no
      model keys, no raw repository credentials, and no secrets.</li>
      <li><strong>OpenFlows</strong> decides how the work is coordinated. It provides the team, the
      planning gate, adversarial review, self-healing, and merges.</li>
    </ul>
    Neither replaces the other. OpenFlows does not manage machines; Coder does not write your code.
  </Callout>

  <StepHeading step="3" id="install">Install &amp; Set Up</StepHeading>
  <p>
    Setting up OpenFlows means standing up the control plane (Coder, the coordination store, and a
    database), running the installer to bootstrap the environment, adding a tenant for your repository,
    and linking GitHub OAuth. From there you can open an issue and watch the team turn it into a merged
    pull request.
  </p>
  <p>
    Step-by-step guidance lives on the <a href="/docs/getting-started/installation">Installation</a>
    page and the <a href="/docs/getting-started/first-issue">First Issue Walkthrough</a>.
  </p>

  <StepHeading step="4" id="track-your-first-issue">Track Your First Issue</StepHeading>
  <p>
    Once the environment is up and your tenant is configured, create a clear GitHub issue in the bound
    repository. OpenFlows does the rest:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>NEXUS picks</strong> the issue up and turns it into a ticket.</li>
    <li><strong>FORGE writes</strong> a plan, then <strong>halts</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>SENTINEL reviews</strong> the plan and approves it.</li>
    <li><strong>FORGE implements</strong>, tests, and opens a pull request.</li>
    <li><strong>SENTINEL reviews</strong> the pull request adversarially.</li>
    <li><strong>VESSEL watches</strong> CI, resolves conflicts, and merges.</li>
    <li><strong>LORE documents</strong> the change (where enabled).</li>
    <li><strong>The workspace is torn down</strong> after merge - no lingering state.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If at any point OpenFlows needs a human call - a security concern, an ambiguous spec, or an
    unresolvable merge - you are notified and the team waits for your decision. See the
    <a href="/docs/getting-started/first-issue">First Issue Walkthrough</a> and the
    <a href="/docs/getting-started/first-issue-lifecycle">Lifecycle</a> pages for the full picture.
  </p>

  <StepHeading step="5" id="what-openflows-is-not">What OpenFlows is not</StepHeading>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not a chat assistant.</strong> It is not a tool you prompt and babysit; it is a team that plans, reviews, and merges under its own guardrails. You decide, it executes.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a hosted cloud coder.</strong> It runs in your own Coder control plane. Code, prompts, and outputs stay in your environment, and worker workspaces hold no keys by design.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a bare orchestrator you maintain.</strong> The planning gate, adversarial review, recovery, and human escalation are already built - you do not write the coordination logic.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a replacement for Coder.</strong> It is a layer on top. It needs your self-hosted Coder environment to run, and it favors governance over raw speed.</li>
  </ul>

  <StepHeading step="6" id="next-steps">Next Steps</StepHeading>
  <DocCards cards={[
    { icon: '🔑', title: 'Identity & OAuth', desc: 'How OpenFlows acts on GitHub through a scoped OAuth link - no raw tokens in workspaces.', href: '/docs/getting-started/token-guide' },
    { icon: '🎯', title: 'Installation', desc: 'What you need before you start, the topology, and what the installer does.', href: '/docs/getting-started/installation' },
    { icon: '🚀', title: 'First Issue Walkthrough', desc: 'Step through one complete ticket from issue creation to merged PR.', href: '/docs/getting-started/first-issue' },
    { icon: '🔁', title: 'Ticket Lifecycle', desc: 'The phases every ticket travels through, from planning to merge.', href: '/docs/getting-started/first-issue-lifecycle' },
    { icon: '❓', title: 'FAQ', desc: 'Short answers to common setup, security, and operational questions.', href: '/docs/faq' },
  ]} />

  <Callout type="info" title="Digging deeper">
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/concepts/coder-integration">Coder Integration</a> - how OpenFlows and Coder split the work</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/concepts/architecture-first">Architecture-First</a> - why OpenFlows plans before coding</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/concepts/agent-team">The Agent Team</a> - who NEXUS, FORGE, SENTINEL, VESSEL, and LORE are</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/concepts/security-model">Security Model</a> - how secrets stay out of worker workspaces</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/guides/operations">Operations</a> - managing the fleet at runtime</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/guides/multi-tenancy">Multi-Tenancy</a> - one Coder server, many teams</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <FeedbackFooter />

  <style>
    .prereqGrid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
      gap: 1.5rem;
      margin: 2rem 0;
    }
  </style>
</DocsLayout>
